1. e4 Nc6 2. Nf3 g6 3. d4 Bg7 4. c3 d6 5. Bc4 Nf6 6. Qe2 O-O 7. Bb3 Nd7 8. O-O
h6 9. Bf4 e5 10. Be3 Kh7 ({The SDN gave} 10... Kh8 {as the move played here.})
11. dxe5 dxe5 12. Kh1 Qe7 13. g4 Nc5 14. Bxc5 Qxc5 15. Rg1 Nd8 16. Nbd2 a5 17.
a3 Ne6 18. Bxe6 Bxe6 19. Rg3 Rad8 20. Rag1 Rd7 21. Rh3 Kg8 22. Rhg3 Rfd8 23. h3
Qc6 24. Kh2 b5 25. Rc1 Bf6 26. Nf1 Bc4 27. Qe1 Bxf1 28. Qxf1 Qxe4 29. Qxb5 Qf4
30. Rf1 e4 31. Nd4 Bh4 32. Kg2 Rd5 (32... Bxg3 33. fxg3 Qd6 34. Qc4 Qd5 35.
Qxd5 Rxd5 36. b4 axb4 37. axb4 c5 {Source: Sussex Daily News, 2 Aug 1928}) 33.
Qc4 Bxg3 34. fxg3 Qd6 35. b4 axb4 36. axb4 c5 37. bxc5 Qxc5 38. Qxc5 Rxc5 39.
Re1 Rxc3 40. Nb5 Rd2+ 41. Kf1 Rf3+ 42. Kg1 Rxg3+ 43. Kf1 Rxh3 {Taken mainly
from the Cheltenham Chronicle of 29 Sept 1928. However, for moves 12 and 13
the move order of the Sussex Daily News of 2 Aug 1928 has been adopted (the CC
has 12 g4 Qe7 13 Kh1 Nc5 which seems less likely to be correct). The SDN
version has 32..Bg3 33 fg3 Qg5 (this may or may not be correct - the original
score gives 33..Q-K4) 34 Qc4 Qd5 35 Qd5 Rd5 36 b4 ab4 37 ab4 c5 'and wins.'
The SDN also has White moving a pawn to Q4 on both moves three and four. Also
published in the Big Database 2013, which adopts the SDN sequence for moves 12
and 13 and then has the SDN ending from 32...Bxg3 except that it gives 33...
Qd6 instead of Qg5 and it ends the game on move 37 without indicating that any
further moves were played. (Brian Denman)  (Updated 21 Apr 2016)} 0-1

